extern int shm_unlink(const char *);
```
So, the proper definitions are `_XPG4_2` and `__EXTENSIONS__` together. Guess, the latter one is defined by Python's source elsewhere already, when it gets to the inclusion of `sys/mman.h` in `Modules/_multiprocessing/posishmem.c` and that is why defining `_XPG4_2` there fixed my issue of `shm_open()` and `shm_unlink()` being out of reach during the building of Python 3.9 and 3.10, but broke the compilation at your command line. (I've tried to define `_POSIX_C_SOURCE` as a bigger number than `2`, but that yielded only an error macro being activated. So, `__EXTENSIONS__` must be defined in tandem with `_XPG4_2`, not `_POSIX_C_SOURCE`.)
As for linking, i forgot, that on Solaris 10, `-lposix4` is required for a lot of POSIX stuff to work. (Sorry about that.) Guess, Python `Makefile` passes that to the linker too, so i did not have to specify it manually during building Python.

So, for the snippet
```
#include <sys/mman.h>

void main(int argc, char const *argv[]) {
	shm_open(NULL, 0, 0);
}
```
the correct compilation line is `cc s10shmtest.c -D_XPG4_2 -D__EXTENSIONS__ -lposix4` and with that, it compiles and links without warnings, or errors under GCC3, 5 and 9 too. (Okay, under GCC3, it does throw a warning about `main()` does not returning an `int`, but that is irrelevant.)

Defining `__EXTENSIONS__` alone will not be enough, because it is not just that one `#if`, there is another above. The block is:
```
#if (_POSIX_C_SOURCE > 2) || defined(_XPG4_2)
extern void *mmap(void *, size_t, int, int, int, off_t);
extern int munmap(void *, size_t);
extern int mprotect(void *, size_t, int);
extern int msync(void *, size_t, int);
#if (!defined(_XPG4_2) || (_POSIX_C_SOURCE > 2)) || defined(__EXTENSIONS__)
extern int mlock(const void *, size_t);
extern int munlock(const void *, size_t);
extern int shm_open(const char *, int, mode_t);
extern int shm_unlink(const char *);
```
Therefore you have to have either `_POSIX_C_SOURCE` set to a number greater than `2`, or define `_XPG4_2` to get the entire block of functions. If you did, you already have `mmap()`, `munmap()`, `mprotect()` and `msync()`, but not `mlock()`, `munlock()`, `shm_open()` and `shm_unlink()`. You would, if you'd get the block by setting `_POSIX_C_SOURCE` to `3` or higher and not defining `_XPG4_2`, but if you do that, that will yield errors triggered by `#error` macros, so you cannot. You only can do this, by leaving `_POSIX_C_SOURCE` alone and define `_XPG4_2` instead to get the entire block, and define `__EXTENSIONS__` to get the inner block too.

In short, getting `shm_open()` and `shm_unlink()` is like `and`-ing the two `if`-s together:
```
#if ((_POSIX_C_SOURCE > 2) || defined(_XPG4_2)) && ((!defined(_XPG4_2) || (_POSIX_C_SOURCE > 2)) || defined(__EXTENSIONS__))
```
Or, in shorter:
```
#if ((_POSIX_C_SOURCE > 2) || (defined(_XPG4_2) && defined(__EXTENSIONS__)))
```
And since `_POSIX_C_SOURCE` must be left alone, it is the latter half: `defined(_XPG4_2) && defined(__EXTENSIONS__)`

