https://github.com/emersion/go-imap/blob/22f41a99378e2d3e7fe67599252c3f97ece9d241/internal/imapwire/encoder.go#L110-L129

```go
func (enc *Encoder) validQuoted(s string) bool {
    if len(s) > 4096 {
        return false
    }

    for i := 0; i < len(s); i++ {
        ch := s[i]

        // NUL, CR and LF are never valid
        switch ch {
        case 0, '\r', '\n':
            return false
        }

        if !enc.QuotedUTF8 && ch > unicode.MaxASCII {
            return false
        }
    }
    return true
}
```
They also seem to only validate NUL, CR and LF. However, that is a different issue that go-imap also allows NUL characters in string literals, through this:
https://github.com/emersion/go-imap/blob/22f41a99378e2d3e7fe67599252c3f97ece9d241/internal/imapwire/encoder.go#L102-L108

```go
func (enc *Encoder) String(s string) *Encoder {
	if !enc.validQuoted(s) {
		enc.stringLiteral(s)
		return enc
	}
	return enc.Quoted(s)
}
```

Which means that they may return a string literal with NUL character when it's not a valid quoted string (which is against the RFC). Their implementation also seems to have this slight fault but other than that, I think they validate for the right control characters here. 

