Ok, I was able to repro the issue. The problem -- or at least one of the problems -- is that when stopping tracemalloc we set the ctx back to NULL. Another thread can see an inconsistency: it may call `tracemalloc_alloc` but without the right context.

It's a lot easier to repro on the free threading build, but the reported crashes are all with the GIL enabled. 

```python
import _testcapi
for i in range(5000):
    _testcapi.tracemalloc_track_race()
```

You can also increase the chance in a GIL-enabled build, by changing `PyMem_RawMalloc` to:

```c
void *
PyMem_RawMalloc(size_t size)
{
    void *ctx = _PyMem_Raw.ctx;
    sched_yield();
    void *(*malloc_func)(void *, size_t) = _PyMem_Raw.malloc;
    return malloc_func(ctx, size);
}
```

I think we can make it a no-op if we use the same value, but I'm not familiar with this module. Checking for whether something can be silently ignored or not requires to call the getters whenever we call the setters. I don't think it'll become slower by much but, ideally, I'd think it's more of an API misuse.

That being said, it's also better if we don't make existing librairies unsable (piper-tts seems to have [30k downloads](https://pypistats.org/packages/piper-tts) per month, not necessarily much but still noticable I'd say). Since the module is also quite niche on our side, I think we can do this kind of work on our side.

